<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonard Bernstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Louis Bernstein, Lenny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonard Bernstein was an American conductor, composer, pianist and music educator, born in Lawrence, Massachusetts, in 1918 to Jewish immigrant parents, and the first American-born conductor to win international acclaim. His unrehearsed debut with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Philharmonic in 1943, broadcast nationwide, made him famous, and he was its music director from 1957, jointly at first, until 1969. He composed the musical West Side Story (1957), On the Town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a> Psalms and the score of On the Waterfront, and he was central to the modern revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a>'s music. His Young People's Concerts brought classical music to television audiences. He died in New York in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonard-bernstein.jpg" alt="Leonard Bernstein" width="330" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jack Mitchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonard_Bernstein_by_Jack_Mitchell_(high_quality).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
